<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="returns-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="returns-title">The returns most small businesses deal with</h2>
      <p>Which ones apply depends on your registration type and turnover. The {% ext "https://www.gst.gov.in/", "GST portal" %} is the final word.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="GST returns, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">What it is, in plain terms</th><th scope="col">What it depends on</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">GSTR-1</th><td>Your statement of sales, invoice by invoice for business customers</td><td>Your sales register, credit and debit notes</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">GSTR-3B</th><td>Your summary return, showing tax due, input tax credit claimed and tax paid</td><td>GSTR-1 figures, your purchases, and credit available</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">GSTR-2B</th><td>A statement generated for you from what suppliers reported. You do not file it, but you rely on it for input tax credit</td><td>Your suppliers filing correctly</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Invoice Management System</th><td>Where you act on invoices that suppliers have reported</td><td>Someone checking them regularly</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">QRMP scheme</th><td>Quarterly returns with monthly tax payment, for eligible smaller taxpayers (reported limit: turnover up to ₹5 crore)</td><td>Your eligibility and choice</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Annual return</th><td>A yearly filing that applies to some taxpayers, depending on the rules for the year</td><td>Twelve months of correct monthly work</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="section-foot">Commonly published due dates are the 11th for monthly GSTR-1 and the 20th for monthly GSTR-3B, but quarterly filers differ, dates can be extended, and we read these on third-party calendars. Check the portal for yours.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="recon-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="recon-title">What a mismatch is telling you</h2>
      <p>This is where a good person earns their fee. If someone only uploads figures and does not look at these, ask why.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="deliver-grid">
      <article><h3>Books and GSTR-1 differ</h3><p>A sale may be missing or entered twice, or a credit note may have been left out.</p></article>
      <article><h3>GSTR-1 and GSTR-3B differ</h3><p>Sales reported in one and not the other can draw attention from the department. There may be a valid timing reason, so ask for it in writing.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Books show credit that GSTR-2B does not</h3><p>The supplier may not have reported the invoice, or may have made an error. Credit on that bill may need to wait, so someone must follow up.</p></article>
      <article><h3>GSTR-2B shows invoices you do not recognise</h3><p>It may be a supplier's mistake, or an entry against your GSTIN that deserves attention. Do not ignore it.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Cash ledger and bank differ</h3><p>A payment may have gone to the wrong head or may not have been set off.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Stock does not support the sales</h3><p>Records may be incomplete. This can matter in an audit or inquiry.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="yours-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="yours-title" class="first-h2">What stays your responsibility</h2>
      <p>Hiring someone does not transfer the legal duty. Section 48(3) of the CGST Act says responsibility for correctness of what a GST practitioner files "shall continue to rest with the registered person". In practice:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Hand over complete sales and purchase data on an agreed date.</li>
        <li>See the figures before filing, and approve them yourself.</li>
        <li>Keep login details and registered contacts in your own name.</li>
        <li>Understand what each OTP approves before you give it.</li>
        <li>Pay tax on time. A filing is not a payment.</li>
        <li>Read portal notices, or be clear who reads them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="who">Who can do this for you</h2>
      <p>Accountants, enrolled GST practitioners and CAs commonly handle GST returns. If someone acts as a GST practitioner, ask for the enrolment details. Rule 83 provides for authorising a practitioner on the portal through Form GST PCT-05, which is safer than sharing your password. Replying to a notice or appearing for you is a different matter. See {% routeLink "/services/gst-notice-litigation/", "GST notices and disputes" %}.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">Questions to ask before hiring</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Which reconciliations do you do each month, and will I see them?</li>
        <li>What date do you need my data by?</li>
        <li>Will I approve figures before filing?</li>
        <li>Who follows up with suppliers over missing credit?</li>
        <li>Is notice work included? If not, how is it charged?</li>
        <li>Do you file through a practitioner authorisation, or using my login?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fees">What affects the fee</h2>
      <p>Invoice volume, purchases to reconcile, number of GSTINs, backlog or late returns, e-commerce or export complications, and whether annual filing and notices are included. AccountOrbit does not set fees. For records to prepare, see {% routeLink "/guides/documents-required-for-gst-return-filing/", "documents for GST return filing" %}.</p>

      <h2 id="specialist">When to get specialist help</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You have received a notice or a demand.</li>
        <li>Returns are months behind, or registration is cancelled.</li>
        <li>You have large or unexplained credit mismatches.</li>
        <li>You are unsure how a supply should be classified or taxed.</li>
      </ul>

